+- **Best for:** Self-hosted product search with strong UX defaults
+
+---
+
+#### [Vespa](https://github.com/vespa-engine/vespa)
+> **Description:** Vespa’s README centers on serving AI and data online with support for search, vectors, tensors, and recommendation serving. That combination is particularly useful in ecommerce when retrieval, ranking, and personalization need to happen in one low-latency system. On GitHub, Vespa showed **6.8k stars**, **Java** as the primary language, and an **Apache-2.0** license. Its repo and releases were active into **Mar 2026**, and it remains one of the strongest open platforms for advanced retrieval plus ranking pipelines. Use it when catalog size, relevance logic, or real-time ranking complexity exceed simpler search engines.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/vespa-engine/vespa](https://github.com/vespa-engine/vespa)
+- **Stars:** 6.8k ⭐
+- **Language:** Java
+- **License:** Apache-2.0
+- **Last Commit:** Mar 2026
+- **Category:** search, ranking
+- **Best for:** High-scale retrieval and ranking systems with vector support
+
+---
+
+#### [Qdrant](https://github.com/qdrant/qdrant)
+> **Description:** Qdrant describes itself as a high-performance vector database and vector search engine for the next generation of AI applications, with recommender-system and hybrid-search topics visible on GitHub. For ecommerce, it is useful for semantic search, embedding-based product similarity, and retrieval-augmented assistants that need fast nearest-neighbor lookup plus metadata filters. GitHub showed **29.9k stars**, **Rust** as the primary language, an **Apache-2.0** license, and active releases in **Mar 2026**. It works especially well as the vector layer beside a transactional catalog or search stack.
